      "gap": "Grinstead and Snell sec. 1.2 (book pp. 26-28, the paragraphs on the choice of sample space and the subsections Determination of Probabilities and Odds) give the source account of how the weights of a finite model are chosen and justified: the uniform law on the head counts 0, 1, 2 is a valid distribution function that is inconsistent with observed coin-tossing data; symmetry suggests the uniform distribution, but equal probabilities are not justified merely because no reason favours one outcome; statistical observation determines weights (the proportion of newborns who are boys is about .513, a figure that may change with new studies and vary between countries); statistical estimates suit experiments repeatable under similar circumstances, and otherwise a personal probability is read from the bets one would accept (giving 2 to 1 odds on Dartmouth corresponds to probability 2/3). def:p15-finite-probability-limits says only that applying stipulated weights to a population needs a justified model and that the identities do not estimate the weights; none of these source claims, nor the empirical claim with its population and stated instability, is supplied on the route.",
